Article 10

(1) Protection of children starts from the womb until the age of 18 (eighteen) years.

(2) Protection of children in the womb, infants and toddlers includes:

a. protection of children in the womb, including ensuring children are born in a healthy condition, to reduce maternal mortality rates and reduce child mortality rates;
b. ensuring every child is born with medical procedures in accordance with SOPs from the Ministry of Health;
c. ensuring every child after birth is conducted with the guidance of the religion adhered to by their parents;
d. provision of complete immunization facilities both at posyandu, puskesmas or government hospitals; e. improving the quality and competence of cadres at Posyandu, to ensure correct procedures in order to ensure the health of mothers and children including the immunization process;
f. provision of proper facilities for every posyandu and Bina Keluarga Balita (BKB) in every kelurahan; g. ensuring the quality of knowledge of posyandu cadres and toddler family development cadres in accordance with government standards;
h. providing education to the community about becoming great parents, to ensure children's health, both at posyandu, toddler family development together with government and community work partners;
i. provision of playgrounds and child care facilities managed by the regional government or organizations under the auspices of the Regional Government;
j. provision of Breastfeeding Corners in offices that have female employees; and
k. provision of attention and affection from the family. (3) Protection for school-age children:
a. prohibiting acts of bullying against children at school;
b. ensuring there are no acts of bullying between children, teachers to children, parents to children and the community to children whether at school, at home or in the community;
c. providing education to schools, families and the community about child-friendly schools;
d. requiring every school at all levels to meet child-friendly school criteria;
e. receiving full attention and affection from family and community;
f. receiving religious guidance and local customs/culture including sumbang 12 (dua baleh);
g. receiving twelve years of compulsory education;
h. providing scholarship programs for underprivileged children and children who excel;
i. providing supporting facilities for extracurricular activities such as for sports subjects, arts and other local content as well as activity supervisor teachers; and
j. prohibiting school children from being at internet cafes during school hours and after 18.00 WIB.
(4) Every person and/or any party is obliged to protect school-age children from acts of violence in the family, school and community environment.
(5) Protection for neglected children:
a. regional government, NGOs/social organizations and the community are obliged to provide protection for neglected children domiciled in the Region in accordance with statutory regulations;
b. regional government ensures children do not live on the streets;
c. protection for neglected children whose parents do not have the ability and/or neglect them as well as children who have no parents, which is implemented through institutional and non-institutional services by the regional government and the community;
d. the form of institutional services as referred to in letter (c), is implemented in a family atmosphere and in the nearest environment of the child managed by the community and in the form of an institution;
e. the form of non-institutional services as referred to in letter (c) is implemented in a family atmosphere managed by the community and not in the form of an institution; and
f. community-owned institutions as referred to in letter (d) must meet the requirements in accordance with applicable statutory regulations.

Article 31

(1) In order to ensure the effectiveness of implementing KLA policies in the Region, a KLA Task Force is established.

(2) The implementation of KLA policies as referred to in paragraph (1) is stipulated by Mayor's Decree.

(3) The KLA Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1) has the principal tasks of:

a. coordinating the implementation of policies and development of KLA;
b. determining the tasks of Task Force members; c. conducting socialization, advocacy and communication of information and education on KLA policies;
d. collecting baseline data;
e. conducting needs analysis based on baseline data;
f. disseminating baseline data;
g. determining the focus and priority of programs in realizing KLA, which are adjusted to regional potential;
h. preparing a 5 (five) year regional action plan for KLA and working mechanisms; and
i. conducting monitoring, evaluation and reporting at least once every 1 (one) year.
(4) Membership of the KLA Task Force is stipulated by Mayor's Decree.

